  "response_text": "Round-robin scheduling using a queue involves enqueueing threads at the end and dequeuing them from the front each time a thread yields, or its time slice ends. When a thread finishes its execution or yields, it is placed back into the queue. Thus, the scheduler simply dequeues the next thread to run, making scheduling very straight forward. The primary benefit is efficiency in insertion and removal operations, as queues naturally support O(1) complexity for these operations. In contrast, array operations like shifting elements can take O(n) time.\\n",
